# `packages/crypto` is frozen unless you are fixing a real integration bug

`packages/crypto` implements the 4AllPass Zero-Knowledge crypto protocol
(`docs/crypto-protocol.md`, `docs/threat-model.md`, `docs/webauthn-prf.md`,
`docs/vault-revision.md`) and has already been through multiple rounds of
adversarial review (`docs/adversarial-review.md`). Treat it as authoritative
and stable.

**Do not modify `packages/crypto` unless required to fix an actual
integration bug** — i.e. the calling code (backend or frontend) cannot
correctly use the package as specified, and the fix is on the
`packages/crypto` side, not the caller's.

Concretely, this means:

- Never touch `packages/crypto` "to simplify" building an unrelated feature
  (auth, sessions, authorization, backend routes, UI). If a task in another
  part of the repo seems to require a crypto change, stop and explain the
  conflict instead of changing the protocol.
- Never redesign the crypto protocol, invent new primitives, change
  AES-256-GCM usage, change the HKDF construction, change the WebAuthn PRF
  construction, or change the DWK/DK/VK key hierarchy.
- Never weaken or bypass Zero-Knowledge guarantees (e.g. adding server-side
  decryption, sending plaintext or key material to the backend).
- Never change existing public wire/crypto formats without an explicit,
  documented protocol version bump.
- If you do fix a genuine integration bug in `packages/crypto`, keep the
  change minimal, add/update the adversarial or unit tests that would have
  caught it, and document the fix and its rationale.
